content:
At dawn I rise and stir the journey bells;
A traveler on the road, I grieve for home.
Cockcrow beneath the moon at the thatched inn;
Footprints in the frost on the plank bridge.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain path;
Trifoliate-orange blossoms brighten the post-station wall.
And so I think of my dream of Duling:
Ducks and geese fill the winding pond.

Notes
- Duling: a place near Chang’an, evoking the home region the traveler longs for; here it is not a person’s name.

At dawn I rise; the travel bells ring;
A traveler on the road, I grieve for home.
At cockcrow, the moon over the thatched inn;
Footprints in frost on the plank bridge.
Oak leaves fall along the mountain path;
Trifoliate-orange blossoms brighten the post-station wall.
Then I recall my dream of Duling:
Ducks and wild geese fill the winding pond.

Notes
Duling: a district near Chang’an, associated here with the poet’s home and memories.

At dawn I rise and set the journey bells ringing;
A traveler on the road, I grieve for my old home.
Cockcrow, the moon over a thatched inn;
Footprints on a plank bridge, frost.
Oak leaves fall on the mountain path;
Trifoliate-orange blossoms brighten the post-station wall.
Then I think of my dream of Duling:
Wild ducks and geese fill the winding pond.

Notes
Duling: a place near Chang’an, evoking the home region the traveler longs for; here it is a place name, not a person’s name.
